From 948c95f26228fe494f9018920854fab45c2090f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:09:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Shift printer replies out MSB first

The printer loop assembles each received byte MSB first, as the Game
Boy sends it, but shifted its replies out LSB first. The Game Boy
samples MSB first, so it read every reply bit-reversed. That has gone
unnoticed only because the two bytes sent, 0x81 (device ID) and 0x00
(status), read the same either way; a real status (0x02 busy, 0x04
full) would arrive as 0x40 or 0x20.

Checked in gbagate's simulator, whose Game Boy samples SO on the rising
edge MSB first: with a status of 0x02, it read 0x40 before this change
and 0x02 after. The printer protocol test passes as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014LvpUYFw94JkaNFyrbwJmK
---
 src/module/gb.cpp | 6 +++---
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/module/gb.cpp b/src/module/gb.cpp
index bd28445..3feaa39 100644
--- a/src/module/gb.cpp
+++ b/src/module/gb.cpp
@@ -362,9 +362,9 @@ void GBModule::printerModeLoop()
             }
         }
 
-        // Clock is LOW — output our bit (LSB first)
-        gpio_put(PIN_SOUT, send_data & 0x1);
-        send_data = send_data >> 1;
+        // Clock is LOW — output our bit (MSB first, as the Game Boy samples it)
+        gpio_put(PIN_SOUT, (send_data >> 7) & 0x1);
+        send_data = static_cast<uint8_t>(send_data << 1);
 
         // Wait for clock to go high
         while (!gpio_get(PIN_SCK)) {}
